\subsubsection{Effect of Multi-Scale Strategy on Despeckling.}
% We further tested various model configurations: (1) three encoders for high, medium, and low-resolution inputs (H+M+L), (2) two encoders for high and low resolutions (H+L), (3) two encoders for medium and low resolutions (M+L), (4) two encoders for high and medium resolutions (H+M), and (5) a single shared encoder for all three inputs (OneEncoder). As shown in TABLE~\ref{tab:ablation}, the H+M+L configuration consistently delivered the best results across all scenarios, underscoring the impact of specific scale combinations on despeckling effectiveness. The H+L configuration underperformed due to the large disparity between high and low resolutions, introducing excessive structural variation that the network struggled to handle. The H+M setup also proved ineffective, as the relatively small difference between high and medium resolutions did not provide sufficient variation to capture distinct speckle patterns. In contrast, the M+L configuration struck a balance, offering an intermediate level of scale difference that enabled the network to leverage cross-scale information. However, the performance is still less than using H+M+L. In addition, the shared encoder setting achieved the second-best performance. This is because using all three scale images ensures the speckle pattern variations in different scales. Due to the same principle, it has the despeckling ability even if it uses only a single encoder.

We further evaluated different model configurations to understand the impact of scale combinations and encoder design:
(1) H+M+L: Three encoders for high, medium, and low resolutions;
(2) H+L: Two encoders for high and low resolutions;
(3) M+L: Two encoders for medium and low resolutions;
(4) H+M: Two encoders for high and medium resolutions;
(5) OneEncoder: A single shared encoder for all scales.
As summarized in TABLE~\ref{tab:ablation}, the H+M+L configuration consistently achieved the best performance across all datasets, confirming that combining inputs across diverse scales facilitates more effective disentanglement of shared structure and scale-sensitive speckle. Among dual-scale variants, M+L outperformed the others, likely due to its moderate resolution gap that introduces sufficient variation in speckle while preserving cross-scale structural alignment. In contrast, H+M provided limited variation, while H+L introduced too much, making inter-scale learning less effective. Interestingly, the OneEncoder setup achieved the second-best results. This suggests that even with a shared encoder, the presence of multi-scale inputs alone allows the network to benefit from cross-scale speckle diversity, though not as effectively as when scale-specific encoders are used.


