\section{The Cosmology} \label{sec:cosmology}
%Ecuaciones

The modified QCD vacuum energy density, as constructed in the previous section, can now be interpreted as a dynamical contribution to the total energy budget of the Universe. To explore the cosmological implications of the proposed modification, in this section, we now turn to its integration within the standard Friedmann framework. Guided by the previous considerations, the modified Polyakov potential introduces an additional term in the energy density, leading to the following form of the Friedmann equation~\cite{Holdom:2010ak}
\begin{equation}
    H^2\equiv\left(\frac{\dot{a}}{a}\right)^2=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\left[\sum_i\rho_i+CH^d\right],
\end{equation}
where $\rho_i$ is for radiation and matter density components (baryonic and DM), $C$ is an appropriate constant and $d$ is the parameter presented previously in Eq. \eqref{eq:u}. In this context, the parameter \( d \) can range from $0$ to $n$, in agreement with standard cosmology and $n$ for a dynamical dark energy. This approach preserves the standard form of General Relativity by incorporating the QCD-induced term as an additional energy component in the Friedmann equations, while keeping the continuity equation unaltered. The term \( C H^d \) behaves as a time-dependent dark energy contribution, whose evolution is governed by the expansion rate of the Universe itself.

Fluids follows the standard continuity equation being only affected the geometric part of Friedmann equation, thus we have
\begin{equation}
    \sum_i\left[\dot{\rho}_i+3H(\rho_i+p_i)\right]=0,
\end{equation}
where $p$ is the pressure which follows the EoS $w=p/\rho$.

In terms of dimensionless variables, it is possible to write 
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    E^2(z)-\chi E(z)^d=\Omega_{0m}(z+1)^3+\Omega_{0r}(z+1)^4.
\end{eqnarray}
In this case, we assume matter and radiation fluids as a species and $\chi\equiv8\pi GC/3 H_0^{2-d}$, $E(z)\equiv H(z)/H_0$. We expect that the corrective term in the equation acts like the causative of the late time acceleration. Notice that when $d=0$, the term $\chi$ takes the role of a cosmological constant.

The Friedmann constraint takes the form
\begin{equation}
    1-\chi=\Omega_{0m}+\Omega_{0r}.
\end{equation}
On the other hand, the deceleration parameter can be computed using the formula
\begin{equation}
    q(z)=\frac{(z+1)}{2E(z)^2}\frac{dE(z)^2}{dz}-1,
\end{equation}
meanwhile the cosmographic jerk parameter is calculated through the equation
\begin{equation}
    j(z)=q(z)\big[2q(z)+1\big]+(1+z)\frac{dq(z)}{dz}.
\end{equation}
Similarly, the effective EoS is given by the expression
\begin{equation}
w_{\mathrm{eff}}(z)=\frac{1}{3}\big[2q(z)-1\big],
\label{eq_weff}
\end{equation}
in terms of the deceleration parameter. This framework sets the stage for the parameter estimation using current observational datasets and enables a direct comparison with the standard cosmological model, as we perform in the next section.

