In the construction of the MDI3Y interactions, we take the values of $\rho_0$, $E_0(\rho_0)$, and $K_0$ to be $0.16$~fm$^{-3}$, $-16$~MeV, and $230$~MeV, respectively.
Next, with different values of $\Lambda_3$, we use GEKKO optimization suite~\cite{beal2018gekko} to minimize the weighted squared difference $\chi^{2}$ between $U_0$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:U0_C}) and the nucleon optical potential data $U_{\mathrm{opt}}$ up to $1$~GeV obtained by Hama \textit{et al.}~\cite{Hama:1990vr,Cooper:1993nx}:
\begin{equation}
\chi^{2} = \sum_{i=1}^{N_{d}} \left( \frac{U_{0,i}-U_{\mathrm{opt},i}}{\sigma_{i}} \right)^{2},
\end{equation}
with constraint of the HVH theorem~\cite{Hugenholtz:1958zz,SATPATHY199985}, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
E_{0}\left(\rho_0\right) = \frac{p_{F_0}^{2}}{2m} +U_{0} \left(\rho_0,p_{F_0}\right).
\end{equation}
The $N_d$ is the number of the experimental data points.
Since there are actually no practical errors $\sigma_i$ here, we assign equal weights to each data point.

