For illustration, we select $\Lambda_3=700$, $1318$, and $3000$~MeV/$c$ as representative cases.
It can be seen from Fig.~\ref{fig:U0_L3} that the Hama's data can be successfully reproduced with different choices of $\Lambda_3$, while their predictions above $1$~GeV could differ significantly.
From Fig.~\ref{fig:U0_L3}, it can be observed that when $\Lambda_3=1318$~MeV/$c$, the predicted $U_0$ provides a nice description of the extrapolation of Hama's data.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:U0inf_L3}, we show the values of $U_0$ at infinitely large nucleon momentum at $\rho_0$, denoted as $U_{0}^{\infty}$, for different $\Lambda_3$, with $U_{0}^{\infty}=55$~MeV when $\Lambda_3=1318$~MeV/$c$.
